-    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 14 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspaces`) manages top-level tenancy, membership defaults, data lifecycle policies, and security guardrails.\n\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable name of the workspace displayed in header and navigation | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at provisioning | Public within workspace; visible to all members and invited guests |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace identifier used in API routes and web paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 64 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable after creation; binds database RLS tenancy context |\n| `default_role` | Default permission tier assigned to newly provisioned local users | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER`, `WORKSPACE_VIEWER`, `WORKSPACE_GUEST` | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER` | Restricts baseline capabilities before explicit project assignments |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Number of days soft-deleted items remain in Trash Studio before automated purge | Integer between 7 and 365 | 30 | Governs compliance data retention; automated purge requires daemon runner |\n| `auto_archive_threshold_days` | Inactivity duration after which closed tickets are automatically archived | Integer between 0 and 730 (0 disables auto-archive) | 90 | Keeps active board queries lightweight; archived tickets remain search-indexable |\n| `max_projects_limit` | Maximum number of active projects allowed within this workspace | Positive integer (1 to 1000) | 50 | Resource cap preventing tenant sprawl and memory exhaustion |\n| `air_gap_enforced` | Strict zero-egress flag blocking all external cloud AI and runner connections | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When enabled, only local Ollama models and internal runners execute |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Comma-separated domain allowlist for user registration and invite cards | List of valid domain strings (e.g. `corp.example.com`) | Empty (Allow all verified) | Rejects non-corporate email addresses at registration gate |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project-level attributes, work item defaults, view presets, and planning dimensions.\n\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Display name of the project | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in command palette and breadcrumbs |\n| `key` | Prefix key for issue identifiers (e.g. `KAN-142`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 characters) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates human-readable issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `description` | Markdown overview and charter for the project | Canonical UTF-8 Markdown (up to 10,000 characters) | Empty | Sanitized server-side; safe Mermaid and media proxying enabled |\n| `default_workflow_id` | Identifier of active workflow managing state transitions | Valid UUID of existing project workflow | Default Kanban Workflow | Determines active transition gates, validators, and AI agent checks |\n| `auto_archive_days` | Project-specific threshold for archiving closed work items | Integer (0 to 365, 0 = inherit workspace) | 0 | Overrides workspace retention for high-velocity software teams |\n| `enable_cycles` | Toggles Agile/Scrum cycle and sprint planning features | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Enables cycle velocity tracking, start/end dates, and rollover |\n| `enable_modules` | Toggles functional module breakdown and progress roll-ups | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Allows hierarchical grouping of work items under functional deliverables |\n| `wip_limits_enforcement` | Behavior when Kanban column WIP limits are exceeded | `warn`, `strict_block`, `disabled` | `warn` | `strict_block` rejects card drop operations exceeding column capacity |\n| `default_assignee_mode` | Initial assignment policy for newly authored work items | `unassigned`, `project_lead`, `creator` | `unassigned` | Prevents orphaned tickets and automates triage assignment |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) defines state groups, transition helper hooks, synchronous validators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `state_name` | Human-readable label for the workflow state | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required (e.g. `In Review`) | Appears on board columns, peek drawers, and transition selectors |\n| `state_group` | Standardized category mapping for cycle metrics | `backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled` | `started` | Used in velocity burn-down and sub-issue progress roll-ups |\n| `helper_type` | Engine used to evaluate transition eligibility | `wasm_validator`, `wasm_mutator`, `agent_gate` | `wasm_validator` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `hook_path` | Target module, wasm bytecode, or agent identifier | File path, slug, or registered agent UUID | Required | Executes within sandboxed environment with strict resource caps |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if transition helper rejects or times out | `BLOCK_TRANSITION`, `WARN_AND_ALLOW`, `PROVISIONAL_HOLD` | `BLOCK_TRANSITION` | Fail-closed invariant preventing invalid transitions into protected states |\n| `execution_timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed wall-clock execution time | 50ms to 30000ms (Sync Wasm: <=250ms) | 100ms | Prevents hanging transitions and thread pool starvation |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Isolation level enforced during helper execution | `wasm` (in-process wasmtime) or `nsjail` (container) | `wasm` | Sandboxed with zero network or filesystem syscall access |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) defines dynamic metadata, computed story points, and sandboxed formulas.\n\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable label displayed on ticket rails and tables | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed alongside execution status badge (`\ud83e\uddea`) |\n| `slug` | Unique field identifier used in formulas and JSON payloads | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and computation engine | `scalar`, `formula`, `wasm`, `nsjail` | `formula` | Determines performance budget and standalone mode availability |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `scalar`, `wasmtime` (Tier A), `nsjail` (Tier B) | `wasmtime` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Token identity provided during script execution | `INVOKING_USER`, `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` | `INVOKING_USER` | `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` requires dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `PROJECT`, `WORKSPACE`, `SERVER` | `PROJECT` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, `json_table` | `text` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen output format | JSON object matching schema specification | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or container script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Comma-separated domain list (e.g. `api.github.com`) | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) configures enterprise identity providers, WebAuthn Passkeys, LDAP trees, and break-glass recovery.\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_type` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP`, `WEBAUTHN`, `TOTP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake and token exchange |\n| `card_enabled` | Master active toggle for the authentication card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled cards reject authentication attempts immediately |\n| `sort_rank` | Order in which login cards appear on `/login` screen | Non-negative integer (lower ranks appear first) | 10 | Allows organizations to prioritize SSO while preserving local admin cards |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | String (Always canonical `\ud83d\udc4dkanban`) | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or spoofing |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint URL | Valid HTTPS URL | None | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth/OIDC application client identifier | ASCII string | None | Public identifier registered with enterprise identity provider |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth/OIDC client secret credential | Encrypted string (write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never exposed over REST API |\n| `ldap_host` | LDAPS directory hostname or IP address | Hostname or IP | None | LDAPS on port 636 or StartTLS required; plaintext LDAP rejected |\n| `ldap_bind_dn` | Distinguished Name used for directory lookup | LDAP DN string (e.g. `cn=admin,dc=corp,dc=com`) | None | Service account with read-only search permissions across user tree |\n| `break_glass_active` | Emergency lockout prevention bypass trigger | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Allows emergency local admin access when SSO is offline |\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `key_name` | Descriptive name of the API token or client tool | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `token_prefix` | Standardized key prefix identifying privilege level | `kb_agt_*`, `kb_doc_*`, `kb_adm_*` | Auto-assigned | Cryptographically identifies target surface before database lookup |\n| `lod_tier` | Default Level of Detail returned in tool responses | `summary`, `standard`, `full` | `standard` | Controls response verbosity; `summary` minimizes agent context consumption |\n| `rate_limit_rpm` | Maximum allowed tool invocations per minute | Integer between 10 and 600 | 120 | Protects database connection pool from runaway autonomous agent loops |\n| `expires_at` | Expiration timestamp for the API key | ISO-8601 Timestamp or `None` | 90 days from creation | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED` |\n| `destructive_gate` | Enables two-stage confirmation for destructive operations | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Requires short-lived confirmation token before permanent deletions |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (1=View, 3=Comment, 31=Member, 255=Admin) | 255 (Full) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 1 restricts scopes to `${surface}:read` |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, and configures token lifetimes.\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `kb_client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforced` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `S256` | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent`, `docs`, `admin`, `read:issues`, `write:issues` | `agent` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (300 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai`) registers model providers, configures write-only API keys, and sets monthly token consumption caps.\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_key` | Underlying AI provider implementation | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama`, `custom` | `gemini` | Selects prompt serializer, tool calling dialect, and streaming pipeline |\n| `priority` | Fallback dispatch order when multiple providers are active | Integer (1 to 100, higher runs first) | 10 | Primary provider attempted first; falls back on rate limit or 5xx |\n| `api_endpoint` | Base URL for API communications | Valid HTTP/HTTPS URL | Official cloud endpoint | Local Ollama uses `http://localhost:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Cryptographic API secret for provider access | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never sent back to browser client |\n| `monthly_token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget ceiling | Non-negative integer (0 = unlimited) | 1,000,000 | Blocks cloud invocations once budget is reached to prevent cost overruns |\n| `default_model` | Model identifier used for sparkle and transition gates | String (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`) | Provider default | Balances reasoning capability against response latency |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model generations | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 0.2 | Low temperatures ensure deterministic responses for code and QA gates |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`) authors system personas, binds autonomous agents to service accounts, and configures execution harnesses.\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `agent_name` | Unique name and identifier for the autonomous agent | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Used in ticket mentions, activity streams, and assignment cards |\n| `system_prompt` | Core instructions, persona guidelines, and security boundaries | UTF-8 Markdown text | Required | Directs model reasoning; stripped of dangerous injection sequences |\n| `execution_harness` | Runtime environment executing agent invocations | `api_direct`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `api_direct` | `cli_pipe` uses zero-shell `agy -p` or `codex -p` in Linux jail |\n| `cli_pipe_binary` | Binary path used for zero-shell CLI pipe harness | Pre-approved binary name (`agy`, `codex`) | `agy` | Executes without shell or bash; communicates strictly over STDIN/STDOUT |\n| `service_principal_id` | Bound service account identity for database RLS | Valid UUID of service principal | Auto-provisioned | Binds database permissions to caller identity via `SET LOCAL` |\n| `repository_access` | Git repository access mode granted to the agent | `none`, `ro_mount`, `ephemeral_clone` | `none` | `ro_mount` mounts repository strictly read-only for forensic QA |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) configures distributed build nodes, mints enrollment tokens, and monitors mTLS heartbeats over dedicated port `:8444`.\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `runner_name` | Unique hostname or identifier of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health dashboard and active job telemetry |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic token for runner registration | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires after 15 minutes (900 seconds max); grants certificate signing request (CSR) |\n| `mtls_cert_fingerprint`| SHA-256 fingerprint of validated client certificate | Hexadecimal SHA-256 hash | Registered upon enroll | Hardware-bound identity verifying mutual TLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Standard enrollment invocation command | Shell command string | Generated on mint | Syntax: `echo \"$TOKEN\" \\| uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `max_concurrent_jobs` | Maximum simultaneous task executions allowed on runner | Integer between 1 and 16 | 2 | Throttles runner workload to prevent resource exhaustion |\n| `heartbeat_timeout_s` | Maximum allowable duration without ping before offline | Integer between 10 and 120 seconds | 30 | Dead runners are marked offline and active jobs rescheduled |\n| `allowed_repo_remotes` | Whitelist of Git repository URLs runner can clone | Comma-separated list of repository URLs | Empty (All authorized) | Prevents runner exploitation for arbitrary network exfiltration |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `standalone_embedded_pg`, `production_multi_container` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Encrypts archive into streaming KBKP or snapshot KBBAK1 format |\n| `auto_backup_enabled` | Recurring automated background snapshot toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Instructs .kb_ops.sock daemon to create automated snapshots |\n| `backup_interval_hours`| Frequency of automated snapshot execution | Integer between 1 and 168 hours | `24` | Governs Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and background IO scheduling |\n| `snapshot_retention_count` | Number of historical snapshot archives retained on server disk | Integer between 1 and 90 | `7` | Purges older snapshots automatically while keeping latest N snapshots |\n| `confirm_overwrite` | Confirmation phrase required for destructive restore | Exact string `\"RESTORE\"` | None | Prevents accidental database erasure during snapshot restoration |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`) | `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` | Isolated storage managed by `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon |\n| `compose_networks` | Network isolation topology for exported compose bundle | 5 networks (`edge_net`, `app_net`, `data_net`, `sandbox_net`, `egress_llm_net`) | 5 networks | Eliminates single-network lateral movement across services |\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`deployment_mode`:** Detects whether \ud83d\udc4dkanban is executing inside a single standalone container (`standalone_embedded_pg`, typically launched via `docker run -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest` with internal PostgreSQL 16) or within a compartmentalized production cluster (`production_multi_container`). In standalone mode, external Redis is optional and background tasks run in-process, whereas multi-container mode unlocks dedicated worker containers and nsjail container isolation.\n- **`passphrase`:** The client-supplied cryptographic passphrase submitted during manual streaming backup download (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`) and database restoration (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be at least 16 characters in length. The server derives an AES-256-GCM symmetric key via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 across 600,000 iterations for KBKP streaming downloads, or Argon2id for KBBAK1 archives.\n- **`auto_backup_enabled`:** Toggles automated recurring background snapshots executed by the root-privileged `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon. When enabled, the daemon captures full PostgreSQL state and object graph assets into `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` using the timestamped `KBBAK1` format without interrupting active HTTP or WebSocket connections.\n- **`backup_interval_hours`:** Configures the interval between automated snapshot runs. The backend permits values between 1 and 168 hours (default 24 hours). This setting determines how frequently the `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon wakes to execute scheduled pg_dump snapshot routines.\n- **`snapshot_retention_count`:** Defines the maximum number of automated snapshot `.kbbak` archives preserved within `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` (configurable from 1 to 90, default 7). When a newly generated snapshot exceeds this threshold, the snapshot manager unlinks the oldest archives while verifying that at least the configured retention count remains accessible.\n- **`confirm_overwrite`:** The mandatory safety gate required by the destructive database restore endpoint (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). The payload must contain the exact uppercase string `\"RESTORE\"`. Any request containing mismatched, lowercase, or omitted confirmation tokens is immediately rejected with HTTP 422 before any database sequence is touched or tables are dropped.\n- **`backup_storage_path`:** The server-side directory where automated `.kbbak` archives reside (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`). This path is protected with restrictive permissions and managed exclusively by the fixed-operation socket daemon.\n- **`compose_networks`:** The 5-network topology exported by `GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export`. Separates traffic across `edge_net` (Caddy TLS ingress), `app_net` (FastAPI backend), `data_net` (PostgreSQL 16 and Redis), `sandbox_net` (untrusted Wasm and runner execution), and `egress_llm_net` (outbound AI provider traffic). Accompanied by a secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Migration Import Studio\nThe Migration Import Studio (`/admin/imports`) provides lossless data ingestion from Plane, Jira, GitHub Issues, Linear, and canonical portable workspace bundles.\n\n![Migration Import Studio Multi-Source Tabs](images/slice4-imports-multi-source-tabs.png)\n\n\n### Migration Import Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `workspace_slug` | Destination workspace tenant identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Active workspace | Attaches imported entities to specified tenant under PostgreSQL RLS |\n| `project_key` | Destination project prefix key (e.g. `KAN`, `JIRA`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Bundle default | Re-keys issues sequentially while preserving historical keys as aliases |\n| `dry_run` | Non-destructive simulation toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Calculates entity diffs inside a database savepoint without persisting |\n| `user_map` | Mapping dictionary from source users to canonical users | JSON dictionary of strings | `{}` | Binds historical authors, assignees, and commenters to active principals |\n| `export_workspace_slug` | Target workspace for generating portable bundles | Existing workspace slug | Current | Exports canonical `.tar.gz` archive containing entities, docs, and links |\n| `export_scrub` | Redacts sensitive secrets and tokens during export | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Scrubs passwords, bearer tokens, and credentials from markdown before export |\n\n![Migration Import Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-imports-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`per_source_endpoints`:** Ingestion is executed via 4 distinct per-platform endpoints: `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/plane` (Plane workspace JSON), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/jira` (Jira Cloud/Server JSON or REST dump with ADF support), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/github` (GitHub Issues REST/CLI JSON), and `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/linear` (Linear JSON archive). Each parser normalizes rich text into sanitized UTF-8 CommonMark while strictly rejecting Unicode lone surrogates.\n- **`workspace_slug` and `project_key`:** Defines the destination tenant context and issue key prefix for the imported dataset. If specified, issues are allocated sequential integer sequence IDs under the destination project key (e.g. `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). If left empty, the importer provisions or resolves the workspace and project names specified within the input bundle.\n- **`dry_run`:** Controls whether database modifications are committed. When set to `true`, the importer runs within a PostgreSQL `SAVEPOINT`, parses all entities, validates foreign keys, checks user mappings, and emits an `ImportDiffReport` before rolling back the savepoint. When set to `false`, the operation executes within an atomic database transaction that commits only upon 100% validation success.\n- **`user_map`:** A JSON dictionary mapping source account identifiers, legacy usernames, or email addresses to canonical \ud83d\udc4dkanban user accounts (e.g. `{\"legacy_dev\": \"alice@example.com\"}`). Unmapped accounts fall back to system defaults, ensuring that ownership, assignments, and historical comments remain traceable.\n- **`legacy_key_aliases`:** Upstream issue keys (such as `JIRA-101`, `GH-88`, `ENG-42`) are automatically extracted and recorded in the `issue_key_aliases` catalog. When queries arrive for obsolete keys (`GET /api/v1/issues/{issue_key}`), API clients receive `200 OK` JSON with header `X-Kanban-Canonical-Key: {canonical_key}`, while browser requests requesting `text/html` receive `HTTP 308 Permanent Redirect` with `Location: /projects/{project_key}/issues/{canonical_key}`.\n- **`export_workspace_slug` and `export_scrub`:** Used by the portable workspace exporter (`POST /api/v1/admin/exports/portable`) to generate a compressed `.tar.gz` bundle. When `scrub=true` (`export_scrub` in UI), secret patterns and authentication tokens are actively redacted from prose fields before packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Admin Diagnostics & Realtime Probes Studio\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio (`/admin/diagnostics`) monitors subsystem health, executes active telemetry probes, and tracks container resource limits.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Admin Diagnostics Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `postgresql_rls` | PostgreSQL connectivity, pool saturation, migrations, RLS | 82 migrations, 100 tables `FORCE RLS` | Continuous | Core persistence and tenant isolation; latency threshold < 15ms |\n| `realtime_hub_outbox` | Transactional outbox relay and dispatch loop | Backlog depth, lag seconds, listener status | Continuous | Event delivery to WebSocket subscribers; lag threshold < 5s |\n| `job_worker_pool` | Background asynchronous task execution engine | Active tasks, worker concurrency, DLQ depth | Continuous | Processes asynchronous jobs; dead-letter queue must equal 0 |\n| `wasm_script_sandbox` | Wasmtime AST compiler and script sandbox execution | Compiler state, memory limits, fuel budget | Continuous | Sandboxed custom fields; fuel caps prevent infinite execution loops |\n| `agent_runtime_providers`| Health and latency of active AI model providers | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama` provider status | Continuous | Informs sparkle completions and AI gates; reports monthly budget |\n| `remote_runner_fleet` | Connected mTLS build runner nodes over port :8444 | Online runner count, heartbeat timestamp | Continuous | Distributed workload execution; heartbeats must arrive within 30s |\n| `rate_limiter` | In-memory token bucket and IP throttle health | Bucket capacity, blocked IP count, error rate | Continuous | Protects API endpoints from volumetric denial-of-service |\n| `realtime_pubsub_probe` | Active PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY latency measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 1.0ms) | On Demand | Validates database pub/sub bus responsiveness |\n| `websocket_ws_probe` | Real WebSocket ping/pong frame roundtrip measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 50ms) | On Demand | Validates client-to-server WebSocket bidirectional messaging |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`postgresql_rls`:** Evaluates database health via `GET /api/v1/admin/diagnostics`. Asserts that the single-head Alembic migration tree is fully applied (82/82 migrations), connection pool saturation remains under 75%, and all 100 application tables enforce `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Connection latency is sampled continuously with healthy thresholds < 15ms.\n- **`realtime_hub_outbox`:** Tracks the transactional outbox relay daemon (`database/outbox/relay.py`). Measures the unprocessed message queue depth, average dispatch lag (healthy < 5s), and PostgreSQL `LISTEN/NOTIFY` channel subscription status. Stalled outboxes trigger immediate administrative alerts.\n- **`job_worker_pool`:** Monitors asynchronous background task executors responsible for webhook dispatch, automated snapshot creation, and audit logging. Reports total active tasks, concurrency utilization, and dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth. Any non-zero DLQ count indicates unhandled job failures requiring inspection.\n- **`wasm_script_sandbox`:** Monitors the in-process Wasmtime runtime executing custom field formulas and transition helpers. Verifies compiler initialization, memory consumption caps (default 32 MiB / 33,554,432 bytes per instance), and fuel budget limits preventing CPU exhaustion.\n- **`agent_runtime_providers`:** Asserts the availability and response latency of registered AI model providers (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`). Reports monthly token consumption against configured budget ceilings, warning administrators before cloud budget caps halt automated transition gates.\n- **`remote_runner_fleet`:** Tracks external execution nodes connected via mutual TLS (mTLS) over dedicated port `:8444`. Reports active runner counts, certificate expiration dates, and heartbeat timestamps. Runners failing to report within 30 seconds are marked offline and their active leases rescheduled.\n- **`rate_limiter`:** Reports the runtime state of in-memory token bucket rate limiters across public and authenticated routes. Tracks blocked request volumes and active IP throttle mappings, ensuring system resilience against abusive traffic.\n- **`realtime_pubsub_probe` and `websocket_ws_probe`:** On-demand latency probes. The pub/sub probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`) injects a synthetic outbox notification and captures `LISTEN/NOTIFY` roundtrip time (typically ~0.38ms). The WebSocket probe initiates a live `/ws` binary ping frame in the operator's browser, measuring connection setup (~13.7ms) and ping/pong roundtrip latency (~0.44ms).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio\nThe Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`) inspects, classifies, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across all registered entities.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Link Auditor Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `audit_ids` | Flagged link audit record UUIDs selected for purge | List of 1 to 200 UUIDs | Mandatory in purge | Scope of destructive link deletion in single atomic transaction |\n| `reason` | Mandatory administrative justification for link purge | String (10 to 500 characters) | Mandatory in purge | Recorded in immutable activity ledger; rejects < 10 chars with 422 |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for inspecting audit record lifecycle | `unpurged`, `purged`, `resolved`, `all` | `unpurged` | Isolates active anomalies from resolved historical records |\n| `reason_filter` | Filters links by formal invalidity category | All 7 `InvalidLinkReason` codes | `ALL` | Isolates specific defect patterns for forensic inspection |\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`audit_scan`:** Triggered via `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit` (or the **Run Integrity Scan** button). Traverses `object_links` and returns summary counts across all 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` codes without acquiring table locks.\n- **`reason_filter`:** Allows administrators to filter detected anomalies across the 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` classes:\n  1. `DANGLING_SOURCE`: Source object UUID does not exist in the universal object spine.\n  2. `DANGLING_TARGET`: Target object UUID does not exist in the database.\n  3. `SOFT_DELETED_ENDPOINT`: One or both endpoints reference an object soft-deleted beyond the retention window.\n  4. `ARCHIVED_POLICY_VIOLATION`: Link connects active and archived entities violating project archiving rules.\n  5. `TYPE_MATRIX_VIOLATION`: Relation violates the registered link type matrix allowlist.\n  6. `CROSS_WORKSPACE_LEAK`: Link connects entities across disjoint workspace boundaries without federation.\n  7. `PERMISSION_SEVERED`: Endpoints span boundaries where caller or entity permissions have been severed.\n- **`audit_ids`:** The explicit list of 1 to 200 audit record UUIDs submitted to `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. Submissions with fewer than 1 or more than 200 items are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`reason`:** The mandatory rationale string required by `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. The string must contain at least 10 non-whitespace characters (e.g. `\"Decommissioning orphaned test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings containing fewer than 10 characters are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`status_filter`:** Selects the display subset in the Studio UI: `unpurged` (flagged items requiring operator intervention), `purged` (historically purged records), `resolved` (links where the underlying anomaly was repaired), or `all`.\n- **`immutable_audit_ledger`:** Every completed purge operation writes an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event to `activity_records`. The response returns `purged_count` and the list of `activity_ids` emitted to the ledger for forensic verification.\n"
+    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 14 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspaces`) manages top-level tenancy, membership defaults, data lifecycle policies, and security guardrails.\n\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable name of the workspace displayed in header and navigation | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at provisioning | Public within workspace; visible to all members and invited guests |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace identifier used in API routes and web paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 64 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable after creation; binds database RLS tenancy context |\n| `default_role` | Default permission tier assigned to newly provisioned local users | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER`, `WORKSPACE_VIEWER`, `WORKSPACE_GUEST` | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER` | Restricts baseline capabilities before explicit project assignments |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Number of days soft-deleted items remain in Trash Studio before automated purge | Integer between 7 and 365 | 30 | Governs compliance data retention; automated purge requires daemon runner |\n| `auto_archive_threshold_days` | Inactivity duration after which closed tickets are automatically archived | Integer between 0 and 730 (0 disables auto-archive) | 90 | Keeps active board queries lightweight; archived tickets remain search-indexable |\n| `max_projects_limit` | Maximum number of active projects allowed within this workspace | Positive integer (1 to 1000) | 50 | Resource cap preventing tenant sprawl and memory exhaustion |\n| `air_gap_enforced` | Strict zero-egress flag blocking all external cloud AI and runner connections | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When enabled, only local Ollama models and internal runners execute |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Comma-separated domain allowlist for user registration and invite cards | List of valid domain strings (e.g. `corp.example.com`) | Empty (Allow all verified) | Rejects non-corporate email addresses at registration gate |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project-level attributes, work item defaults, view presets, and planning dimensions.\n\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Display name of the project | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in command palette and breadcrumbs |\n| `key` | Prefix key for issue identifiers (e.g. `KAN-142`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 characters) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates human-readable issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `description` | Markdown overview and charter for the project | Canonical UTF-8 Markdown (up to 10,000 characters) | Empty | Sanitized server-side; safe Mermaid and media proxying enabled |\n| `default_workflow_id` | Identifier of active workflow managing state transitions | Valid UUID of existing project workflow | Default Kanban Workflow | Determines active transition gates, validators, and AI agent checks |\n| `auto_archive_days` | Project-specific threshold for archiving closed work items | Integer (0 to 365, 0 = inherit workspace) | 0 | Overrides workspace retention for high-velocity software teams |\n| `enable_cycles` | Toggles Agile/Scrum cycle and sprint planning features | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Enables cycle velocity tracking, start/end dates, and rollover |\n| `enable_modules` | Toggles functional module breakdown and progress roll-ups | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Allows hierarchical grouping of work items under functional deliverables |\n| `wip_limits_enforcement` | Behavior when Kanban column WIP limits are exceeded | `warn`, `strict_block`, `disabled` | `warn` | `strict_block` rejects card drop operations exceeding column capacity |\n| `default_assignee_mode` | Initial assignment policy for newly authored work items | `unassigned`, `project_lead`, `creator` | `unassigned` | Prevents orphaned tickets and automates triage assignment |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) defines state groups, transition helper hooks, synchronous validators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `state_name` | Human-readable label for the workflow state | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required (e.g. `In Review`) | Appears on board columns, peek drawers, and transition selectors |\n| `state_group` | Standardized category mapping for cycle metrics | `backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled` | `started` | Used in velocity burn-down and sub-issue progress roll-ups |\n| `helper_type` | Engine used to evaluate transition eligibility | `wasm_validator`, `wasm_mutator`, `agent_gate` | `wasm_validator` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `hook_path` | Target module, wasm bytecode, or agent identifier | File path, slug, or registered agent UUID | Required | Executes within sandboxed environment with strict resource caps |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if transition helper rejects or times out | `BLOCK_TRANSITION`, `WARN_AND_ALLOW`, `PROVISIONAL_HOLD` | `BLOCK_TRANSITION` | Fail-closed invariant preventing invalid transitions into protected states |\n| `execution_timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed wall-clock execution time | 50ms to 30000ms (Sync Wasm: <=250ms) | 100ms | Prevents hanging transitions and thread pool starvation |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Isolation level enforced during helper execution | `wasm` (in-process wasmtime) or `nsjail` (container) | `wasm` | Sandboxed with zero network or filesystem syscall access |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) defines dynamic metadata, computed story points, and sandboxed formulas.\n\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable label displayed on ticket rails and tables | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed alongside execution status badge (`\ud83e\uddea`) |\n| `slug` | Unique field identifier used in formulas and JSON payloads | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and computation engine | `scalar`, `formula`, `wasm`, `nsjail` | `formula` | Determines performance budget and standalone mode availability |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `scalar`, `wasmtime` (Tier A), `nsjail` (Tier B) | `wasmtime` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Token identity provided during script execution | `INVOKING_USER`, `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` | `INVOKING_USER` | `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` requires dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `PROJECT`, `WORKSPACE`, `SERVER` | `PROJECT` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, `json_table` | `text` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen output format | JSON object matching schema specification | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or container script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Comma-separated domain list (e.g. `api.github.com`) | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) and Granular RBAC Policy Builder configure enterprise identity providers, Active Directory / LDAPS trees, OpenID Connect SSO, WebAuthn Passkeys, TOTP multi-factor authentication, break-glass lockout prevention, and 30-permission custom roles.\n\n![Admin Authentication Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice1-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_kind` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake, secret custody, and token exchange |\n| `slug` | URL-safe card identifier used in auth routes | Lowercase ASCII `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` (3 to 48 chars) | Required | Formats callback and login endpoints (e.g. `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/start`) |\n| `display_name` | User-facing card title rendered on `/login` screen | UTF-8 String (1 to 100 characters) | Required | Displayed directly on login buttons and cards |\n| `button_subtext` | Optional explanatory subtext beneath button | UTF-8 String (up to 200 characters) or null | None | Guides users on credential format or organizational tenant |\n| `icon_preset` | Visual icon rendered inside login card button | `key`, `shield`, `google`, `github`, `microsoft`, `ldap`, `terminal` | `key` | Provides immediate visual recognition on `/login` container |\n| `is_enabled` | Master active state toggle for the card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Disabled cards reject login attempts immediately with 403 FORBIDDEN |\n| `sort_rank` | Fractional rank ordering on `/login` screen | Non-negative numeric string (e.g. `\"10\"`, `\"20\"`) | `\"50\"` | Lower ranks appear first; allows prioritizing corporate SSO over fallback admin |\n| `auto_provision_users` | Automatic local user creation on first SSO login | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When true, new users are provisioned in workspace with default role |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Domain allowlist for user registration and SSO | Array of domain strings (e.g. `[\"corp.example.com\"]`) | Empty list (Allow all) | Rejects unmatched email claims during OIDC and LDAP authentication |\n| `public_config` | Non-sensitive provider configuration dictionary | JSON object | `{}` | Exposes discovery URLs and client IDs without leaking secrets |\n| `secret` | Sensitive client secret or bind password | Write-only string | None | Encrypted at rest via AES-256-GCM envelope encryption (`MASTER_KEK`) |\n| `google_oauth_json` | Google Cloud Console client secret JSON | Valid JSON string (up to 32,768 characters) | None | Quick-import payload automatically parsed and verified by backend |\n\n\n### Active Directory and LDAPS Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `ldap_host` | Directory server FQDN or IP address | Hostname or IP string | Required | Plaintext LDAP rejected; encrypted LDAPS or StartTLS mandatory |\n| `ldap_port` | Directory service network port | Integer (1 to 65535) | `636` for LDAPS, `389` for StartTLS | Directs TLS connection to domain controller directory services |\n| `ldap_security_mode` | Cryptographic transport security protocol | `ldaps` (SSL/TLS), `starttls` | `ldaps` | Enforces TLS handshake before transmitting bind credentials |\n| `ca_cert_pem` | Custom enterprise Certificate Authority bundle | PEM-formatted X.509 certificate string | None | Pins enterprise root CA; rejects untrusted self-signed certificates |\n| `bind_dn` | Service account Distinguished Name | LDAP DN string (e.g. `CN=svc-kanban,OU=ServiceAccounts,DC=corp,DC=com`) | Required | Read-only service principal used for user and group tree searches |\n| `base_dn` | User search subtree root Distinguished Name | LDAP DN string (e.g. `OU=Users,DC=corp,DC=com`) | Required | Restricts user search scope to authorized organizational units |\n| `user_search_filter` | LDAP query filter for user account resolution | Filter expression matching `{username}` | `(&(objectCategory=person)(sAMAccountName={username}))` | Resolves login username to Active Directory user object |\n| `group_search_base` | Group search subtree root Distinguished Name | LDAP DN string (e.g. `OU=Groups,DC=corp,DC=com`) | Base DN | Restricts security group search scope for RBAC mapping |\n| `group_search_filter` | LDAP query filter for group membership resolution | Filter expression matching `{user_dn}` | `(&(objectCategory=group)(member:1.2.840.113556.1.4.1941:={user_dn}))` | Traverses Active Directory recursive nested groups via `memberOf` |\n| `sync_nested_groups` | Enables LDAP matching rule in chain recursion | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Expands transitive nested Active Directory group memberships |\n\n\n### OpenID Connect (OIDC) and Google OAuth Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint base URL | Valid HTTPS URL | Required | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic JWKS keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth application client identifier | ASCII string | Required | Identifies application to identity provider authorization server |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth application client secret | Write-only secret string | Required | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never returned in REST responses |\n| `scopes` | OIDC OAuth scope parameters | Space-delimited string (e.g. `\"openid email profile\"`) | `\"openid email profile\"` | Must include `openid` and `email` for verified claim extraction |\n| `redirect_uri` | Callback endpoint registered with IdP | Fixed format `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback` | Derived | Enforces strict origin matching against current deployment URL |\n| `require_email_verified`| Rejects unverified IdP email addresses | Boolean (`true`) | `true` | If `email_verified: false`, rejects authentication with 403 EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED |\n\n\n### Personal Security and Multi-Factor Authentication Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | Canonical string `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or relay attacks |\n| `totp_step_seconds` | RFC 6238 TOTP time step interval | Integer | `30` | Validates time-based one-time password window with +/- 1 step drift |\n| `recovery_codes_count` | Number of backup recovery codes generated | Integer | `8` | Single-use high-entropy emergency codes stored as salted hashes |\n| `websudo_duration_seconds` | Duration of elevated AAL2 administrator session | Integer (seconds) | `900` (15 minutes) | Sliding window for sensitive mutations; explicit drop via `/aal2/drop` |\n| `lockout_prevention_layer`| Anti-lockout verification enforcement | `db_trigger`, `handshake_probe_15m` | Both active | Blocks disabling local admin without verified alternative within 15 min |\n\n\n### Granular RBAC Policy Builder Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `scope_tier` | Administrative scope tier of the role | `system admin`, `workspace admin`, `project admin` | `workspace admin` | Governs role hierarchy and locks lower-tier admins from editing parent roles |\n| `role_slug` | Unique lowercase identifier for custom role | Lowercase ASCII `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` | Required | Identifies role in database bindings and audit ledger records |\n| `role_name` | Human-readable role title | UTF-8 String (1 to 100 characters) | Required | Displayed on user management surfaces, member cards, and badges |\n| `color_hex` | UI badge accent color | 7-character hex code (e.g. `#4f46e5`) | `#6b7280` | Distinguishes role visually across member tables and peek drawers |\n| `permissions` | List of granted granular permissions | Array of permission strings from 30 canonical keys | `[]` | Evaluated with explicit DENY precedence (`effective = allow & (~deny)`) |\n\n![Custom RBAC Policy Builder - Desktop](images/slice1-custom-rbac-policy-builder.png)\n\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **provider_kind:** Specifies the authentication protocol implemented by the card. Permitted values are `LOCAL_ADMIN` (argon2id hashed platform administrative credentials), `OIDC` (OpenID Connect discovery and authorization code flow), `OAUTH2` (generic OAuth 2.0 provider with userinfo endpoint), and `LDAP` (Active Directory and OpenLDAP directory integration). The provider kind determines schema validation rules, secret custody requirements, and token minting behavior.\n- **slug:** The unique URL-safe identifier for the authentication card. Must conform to `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` between 3 and 48 characters. The slug forms the path parameter in public login and callback routes, including `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/start`, `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback`, and `/api/v1/auth/ldap/{slug}/login`. Immutable after creation to maintain bookmark and redirect stability.\n- **display_name:** The user-facing label rendered on the login card button on `/login` (1 to 100 characters). For example, `\"Corporate Active Directory\"` or `\"Google Workspace SSO\"`. Provides clear identification of the identity source for end users.\n- **button_subtext:** Optional secondary guidance rendered in smaller typography directly below the card title (up to 200 characters). Commonly used to specify login formats, such as `\"Use your firstname.lastname@example.com account\"` or `\"Hardware security key required\"`.\n- **icon_preset:** Visual icon token rendered on the authentication card button. Supported values include `key` (generic credential), `shield` (security key / passkey), `google` (Google colored G), `github` (GitHub octocat), `microsoft` (Windows logo), `ldap` (directory tree), and `terminal` (administrative root). Enhances visual scannability on multi-card login surfaces.\n- **is_enabled:** Master toggle controlling whether the authentication card is active on the `/login` screen. When set to `false`, the card is hidden from the login container, and any authentication attempts targeting the card's routes are immediately rejected with HTTP 403 `FORBIDDEN`. Mutations toggling `is_enabled` enforce anti-lockout validation.\n- **sort_rank:** Fractional rank order string governing the visual sequence of cards on the `/login` container. Cards with lower numeric ranks appear first. Administrators use this setting to position primary corporate SSO providers at the top while keeping break-glass local admin cards accessible at the bottom.\n- **auto_provision_users:** When enabled (`true`), an authenticated user whose external identity claim is verified but does not yet have an account in \ud83d\udc4dkanban is automatically provisioned with a new local user record and assigned the workspace default role. When disabled (`false`), external users must be explicitly invited before logging in.\n- **allowed_email_domains:** A security restriction array of permitted corporate email domains (e.g., `[\"corp.example.com\", \"subsidiary.com\"]`). During OIDC token exchange or LDAP authentication, if the user's verified email address domain does not match any entry in this list, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `DOMAIN_NOT_ALLOWED`.\n- **public_config:** A JSON dictionary containing non-sensitive provider configuration attributes. Stored in PostgreSQL `jsonb` columns and safe to expose to administrative API callers. Includes discovery URLs, client IDs, LDAP hosts, user search filters, and group mapping definitions.\n- **secret:** Client secrets, LDAP bind passwords, and private key strings. Inbound payloads accept this write-only parameter, which is immediately encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM envelope encryption with the platform `MASTER_KEK`. The secret is never returned in any REST API response or serialization model.\n- **google_oauth_json:** A convenience payload accepting the raw client secret JSON file downloaded directly from the Google Cloud Console (up to 32,768 characters). The backend parses the JSON, validates client ID, client secret, redirect URI alignment, and populates `public_config` without manual copy-pasting.\n- **ldap_host:** The fully qualified domain name or IP address of the target directory server. Plaintext LDAP on unencrypted port 389 is rejected by backend security policies; all connections must negotiate TLS.\n- **ldap_port:** Network port for directory services. Defaults to port `636` for LDAPS (implicit SSL/TLS) and port `389` when `ldap_security_mode` is set to `starttls`.\n- **ldap_security_mode:** Transport encryption mode for directory traffic. Permitted values are `ldaps` (direct TLS handshake upon TCP socket connection on port 636) and `starttls` (explicit StartTLS extended operation negotiation on port 389 prior to issuing bind requests).\n- **ca_cert_pem:** Custom PEM-formatted X.509 certificate authority bundle. When connecting to enterprise directory controllers utilizing private internal PKI, administrators paste the issuing root and intermediate CA certificates here. The LDAP client validates the server certificate strictly against this bundle, rejecting untrusted certificates.\n- **bind_dn:** The Distinguished Name of the service account used by \ud83d\udc4dkanban to execute user lookup and group resolution queries (e.g., `CN=KanbanService,OU=ServiceAccounts,DC=corp,DC=com`). Must possess read-only search permissions over the target user and group subtrees.\n- **base_dn:** The root Distinguished Name of the directory subtree searched for user accounts (e.g., `OU=Employees,DC=corp,DC=com`). Scopes directory queries to prevent scanning unrelated machine or organizational containers.\n- **user_search_filter:** LDAP filter expression utilized to resolve the input login username to a specific Active Directory user object. The token `{username}` is safely escaped and substituted into the filter (e.g., `(&(objectCategory=person)(sAMAccountName={username}))` or `(&(objectCategory=person)(userPrincipalName={username}))`).\n- **group_search_base:** The root Distinguished Name of the directory subtree containing security groups. Used during group synchronization to locate groups mapped to \ud83d\udc4dkanban roles.\n- **group_search_filter:** LDAP filter expression utilized to determine all security groups containing the authenticated user. In Active Directory environments, setting this to `(&(objectCategory=group)(member:1.2.840.113556.1.4.1941:={user_dn}))` leverages the LDAP Matching Rule in Chain (`LDAP_MATCHING_RULE_IN_CHAIN`) to traverse nested group memberships recursively.\n- **sync_nested_groups:** Boolean toggle enabling automatic recursive resolution of nested Active Directory groups. When enabled, users inherit \ud83d\udc4dkanban roles associated with parent groups even if their account is only directly assigned to a child group in the directory.\n- **issuer_url:** The standard OpenID Connect discovery base URL (e.g., `https://accounts.google.com` or `https://keycloak.corp.example.com/realms/enterprise`). The backend appends `/.well-known/openid-configuration` to retrieve endpoint URLs, signing algorithms, and JSON Web Key Sets (JWKS).\n- **client_id:** The public OAuth client identifier issued by the identity provider during application registration.\n- **client_secret:** The confidential OAuth client secret credential issued by the identity provider. Held strictly in write-only custody and encrypted via AES-256-GCM.\n- **scopes:** Space-separated OAuth scope list requested during authorization. Defaults to `\"openid email profile\"`. Scopes must guarantee delivery of the `sub` and `email` claims.\n- **redirect_uri:** The immutable callback URI derived from the deployment hostname and card slug: `https://<domain>/api/v1/auth/oidc/<slug>/callback`. Must be registered verbatim in the identity provider console.\n- **require_email_verified:** Mandatory boolean safety check. When true, the ID token claim `email_verified` must be strictly `true`. If the claim is `false` or missing, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED` to prevent email spoofing attacks.\n- **rp_name:** WebAuthn Relying Party display name. Hardcoded to the canonical platform brand `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` across all WebAuthn registration and authentication credential requests. Displayed by operating system biometric dialogs and hardware tokens.\n- **rp_id:** WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier. Formatted as a strict ASCII Fully Qualified Domain Name matching the browser window origin without protocol, port, or trailing slashes (e.g., `kanban.example.com`). Prevents cross-domain credential phishing.\n- **totp_secret:** High-entropy base32 encoded secret key generated during TOTP authenticator enrollment (`POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/totp/enroll`). Encrypted at rest and used to validate 6-digit RFC 6238 time-step codes.\n- **recovery_codes:** Array of single-use backup recovery codes minted during MFA enrollment. Stored in PostgreSQL exclusively as cryptographic salted hashes. Used for break-glass account recovery when hardware tokens or authenticator apps are unavailable.\n- **websudo_duration_seconds:** The lifetime of an elevated Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) session window (default 900 seconds / 15 minutes). All administrative endpoints (`/api/v1/admin/*`) require active AAL2 verification. When expired, the frontend prompts for step-up elevation via WebSudo without discarding dirty form state.\n- **lockout_prevention_layer:** Multi-layered defense preventing administrative lockouts. Evaluated at both the PostgreSQL trigger layer (`db_trigger`) and application service layer (`handshake_probe_15m`). Prohibits disabling or deleting the last active administrator card unless an alternative external card has passed a live handshake probe within the last 15 minutes (`HANDSHAKE_PROBE_MAX_AGE_SECONDS = 900`).\n- **rbac_scope_tier:** The administrative boundary defining where a custom RBAC role applies. Allowed values are `system admin` (global platform governance), `workspace admin` (workspace-wide settings, members, and custom fields), and `project admin` (project-specific workflows, WIP limits, and issue operations). Roles in higher tiers cannot be edited by administrators in lower tiers.\n- **rbac_permissions:** The set of granular permissions assigned to a custom RBAC role, selected from the 30 canonical keys across Workspace (8), Issue (10), and Field (12) tiers. Evaluated alongside the 8-bit capability bitmask with absolute DENY precedence (`effective = allow & (~deny)`).\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `key_name` | Descriptive name of the API token or client tool | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `token_prefix` | Standardized key prefix identifying privilege level | `kb_agt_*`, `kb_doc_*`, `kb_adm_*` | Auto-assigned | Cryptographically identifies target surface before database lookup |\n| `lod_tier` | Default Level of Detail returned in tool responses | `summary`, `standard`, `full` | `standard` | Controls response verbosity; `summary` minimizes agent context consumption |\n| `rate_limit_rpm` | Maximum allowed tool invocations per minute | Integer between 10 and 600 | 120 | Protects database connection pool from runaway autonomous agent loops |\n| `expires_at` | Expiration timestamp for the API key | ISO-8601 Timestamp or `None` | 90 days from creation | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED` |\n| `destructive_gate` | Enables two-stage confirmation for destructive operations | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Requires short-lived confirmation token before permanent deletions |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (1=View, 3=Comment, 31=Member, 255=Admin) | 255 (Full) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 1 restricts scopes to `${surface}:read` |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, and configures token lifetimes.\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `kb_client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforced` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `S256` | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent`, `docs`, `admin`, `read:issues`, `write:issues` | `agent` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (300 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai`) registers model providers, configures write-only API keys, and sets monthly token consumption caps.\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_key` | Underlying AI provider implementation | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama`, `custom` | `gemini` | Selects prompt serializer, tool calling dialect, and streaming pipeline |\n| `priority` | Fallback dispatch order when multiple providers are active | Integer (1 to 100, higher runs first) | 10 | Primary provider attempted first; falls back on rate limit or 5xx |\n| `api_endpoint` | Base URL for API communications | Valid HTTP/HTTPS URL | Official cloud endpoint | Local Ollama uses `http://localhost:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Cryptographic API secret for provider access | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never sent back to browser client |\n| `monthly_token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget ceiling | Non-negative integer (0 = unlimited) | 1,000,000 | Blocks cloud invocations once budget is reached to prevent cost overruns |\n| `default_model` | Model identifier used for sparkle and transition gates | String (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`) | Provider default | Balances reasoning capability against response latency |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model generations | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 0.2 | Low temperatures ensure deterministic responses for code and QA gates |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`) authors system personas, binds autonomous agents to service accounts, and configures execution harnesses.\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `agent_name` | Unique name and identifier for the autonomous agent | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Used in ticket mentions, activity streams, and assignment cards |\n| `system_prompt` | Core instructions, persona guidelines, and security boundaries | UTF-8 Markdown text | Required | Directs model reasoning; stripped of dangerous injection sequences |\n| `execution_harness` | Runtime environment executing agent invocations | `api_direct`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `api_direct` | `cli_pipe` uses zero-shell `agy -p` or `codex -p` in Linux jail |\n| `cli_pipe_binary` | Binary path used for zero-shell CLI pipe harness | Pre-approved binary name (`agy`, `codex`) | `agy` | Executes without shell or bash; communicates strictly over STDIN/STDOUT |\n| `service_principal_id` | Bound service account identity for database RLS | Valid UUID of service principal | Auto-provisioned | Binds database permissions to caller identity via `SET LOCAL` |\n| `repository_access` | Git repository access mode granted to the agent | `none`, `ro_mount`, `ephemeral_clone` | `none` | `ro_mount` mounts repository strictly read-only for forensic QA |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) configures distributed build nodes, mints enrollment tokens, and monitors mTLS heartbeats over dedicated port `:8444`.\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `runner_name` | Unique hostname or identifier of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health dashboard and active job telemetry |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic token for runner registration | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires after 15 minutes (900 seconds max); grants certificate signing request (CSR) |\n| `mtls_cert_fingerprint`| SHA-256 fingerprint of validated client certificate | Hexadecimal SHA-256 hash | Registered upon enroll | Hardware-bound identity verifying mutual TLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Standard enrollment invocation command | Shell command string | Generated on mint | Syntax: `echo \"$TOKEN\" \\| uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `max_concurrent_jobs` | Maximum simultaneous task executions allowed on runner | Integer between 1 and 16 | 2 | Throttles runner workload to prevent resource exhaustion |\n| `heartbeat_timeout_s` | Maximum allowable duration without ping before offline | Integer between 10 and 120 seconds | 30 | Dead runners are marked offline and active jobs rescheduled |\n| `allowed_repo_remotes` | Whitelist of Git repository URLs runner can clone | Comma-separated list of repository URLs | Empty (All authorized) | Prevents runner exploitation for arbitrary network exfiltration |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `standalone_embedded_pg`, `production_multi_container` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Encrypts archive into streaming KBKP or snapshot KBBAK1 format |\n| `auto_backup_enabled` | Recurring automated background snapshot toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Instructs .kb_ops.sock daemon to create automated snapshots |\n| `backup_interval_hours`| Frequency of automated snapshot execution | Integer between 1 and 168 hours | `24` | Governs Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and background IO scheduling |\n| `snapshot_retention_count` | Number of historical snapshot archives retained on server disk | Integer between 1 and 90 | `7` | Purges older snapshots automatically while keeping latest N snapshots |\n| `confirm_overwrite` | Confirmation phrase required for destructive restore | Exact string `\"RESTORE\"` | None | Prevents accidental database erasure during snapshot restoration |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`) | `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` | Isolated storage managed by `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon |\n| `compose_networks` | Network isolation topology for exported compose bundle | 5 networks (`edge_net`, `app_net`, `data_net`, `sandbox_net`, `egress_llm_net`) | 5 networks | Eliminates single-network lateral movement across services |\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`deployment_mode`:** Detects whether \ud83d\udc4dkanban is executing inside a single standalone container (`standalone_embedded_pg`, typically launched via `docker run -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest` with internal PostgreSQL 16) or within a compartmentalized production cluster (`production_multi_container`). In standalone mode, external Redis is optional and background tasks run in-process, whereas multi-container mode unlocks dedicated worker containers and nsjail container isolation.\n- **`passphrase`:** The client-supplied cryptographic passphrase submitted during manual streaming backup download (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`) and database restoration (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be at least 16 characters in length. The server derives an AES-256-GCM symmetric key via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 across 600,000 iterations for KBKP streaming downloads, or Argon2id for KBBAK1 archives.\n- **`auto_backup_enabled`:** Toggles automated recurring background snapshots executed by the root-privileged `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon. When enabled, the daemon captures full PostgreSQL state and object graph assets into `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` using the timestamped `KBBAK1` format without interrupting active HTTP or WebSocket connections.\n- **`backup_interval_hours`:** Configures the interval between automated snapshot runs. The backend permits values between 1 and 168 hours (default 24 hours). This setting determines how frequently the `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon wakes to execute scheduled pg_dump snapshot routines.\n- **`snapshot_retention_count`:** Defines the maximum number of automated snapshot `.kbbak` archives preserved within `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` (configurable from 1 to 90, default 7). When a newly generated snapshot exceeds this threshold, the snapshot manager unlinks the oldest archives while verifying that at least the configured retention count remains accessible.\n- **`confirm_overwrite`:** The mandatory safety gate required by the destructive database restore endpoint (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). The payload must contain the exact uppercase string `\"RESTORE\"`. Any request containing mismatched, lowercase, or omitted confirmation tokens is immediately rejected with HTTP 422 before any database sequence is touched or tables are dropped.\n- **`backup_storage_path`:** The server-side directory where automated `.kbbak` archives reside (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`). This path is protected with restrictive permissions and managed exclusively by the fixed-operation socket daemon.\n- **`compose_networks`:** The 5-network topology exported by `GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export`. Separates traffic across `edge_net` (Caddy TLS ingress), `app_net` (FastAPI backend), `data_net` (PostgreSQL 16 and Redis), `sandbox_net` (untrusted Wasm and runner execution), and `egress_llm_net` (outbound AI provider traffic). Accompanied by a secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Migration Import Studio\nThe Migration Import Studio (`/admin/imports`) provides lossless data ingestion from Plane, Jira, GitHub Issues, Linear, and canonical portable workspace bundles.\n\n![Migration Import Studio Multi-Source Tabs](images/slice4-imports-multi-source-tabs.png)\n\n\n### Migration Import Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `workspace_slug` | Destination workspace tenant identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Active workspace | Attaches imported entities to specified tenant under PostgreSQL RLS |\n| `project_key` | Destination project prefix key (e.g. `KAN`, `JIRA`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Bundle default | Re-keys issues sequentially while preserving historical keys as aliases |\n| `dry_run` | Non-destructive simulation toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Calculates entity diffs inside a database savepoint without persisting |\n| `user_map` | Mapping dictionary from source users to canonical users | JSON dictionary of strings | `{}` | Binds historical authors, assignees, and commenters to active principals |\n| `export_workspace_slug` | Target workspace for generating portable bundles | Existing workspace slug | Current | Exports canonical `.tar.gz` archive containing entities, docs, and links |\n| `export_scrub` | Redacts sensitive secrets and tokens during export | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Scrubs passwords, bearer tokens, and credentials from markdown before export |\n\n![Migration Import Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-imports-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`per_source_endpoints`:** Ingestion is executed via 4 distinct per-platform endpoints: `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/plane` (Plane workspace JSON), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/jira` (Jira Cloud/Server JSON or REST dump with ADF support), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/github` (GitHub Issues REST/CLI JSON), and `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/linear` (Linear JSON archive). Each parser normalizes rich text into sanitized UTF-8 CommonMark while strictly rejecting Unicode lone surrogates.\n- **`workspace_slug` and `project_key`:** Defines the destination tenant context and issue key prefix for the imported dataset. If specified, issues are allocated sequential integer sequence IDs under the destination project key (e.g. `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). If left empty, the importer provisions or resolves the workspace and project names specified within the input bundle.\n- **`dry_run`:** Controls whether database modifications are committed. When set to `true`, the importer runs within a PostgreSQL `SAVEPOINT`, parses all entities, validates foreign keys, checks user mappings, and emits an `ImportDiffReport` before rolling back the savepoint. When set to `false`, the operation executes within an atomic database transaction that commits only upon 100% validation success.\n- **`user_map`:** A JSON dictionary mapping source account identifiers, legacy usernames, or email addresses to canonical \ud83d\udc4dkanban user accounts (e.g. `{\"legacy_dev\": \"alice@example.com\"}`). Unmapped accounts fall back to system defaults, ensuring that ownership, assignments, and historical comments remain traceable.\n- **`legacy_key_aliases`:** Upstream issue keys (such as `JIRA-101`, `GH-88`, `ENG-42`) are automatically extracted and recorded in the `issue_key_aliases` catalog. When queries arrive for obsolete keys (`GET /api/v1/issues/{issue_key}`), API clients receive `200 OK` JSON with header `X-Kanban-Canonical-Key: {canonical_key}`, while browser requests requesting `text/html` receive `HTTP 308 Permanent Redirect` with `Location: /projects/{project_key}/issues/{canonical_key}`.\n- **`export_workspace_slug` and `export_scrub`:** Used by the portable workspace exporter (`POST /api/v1/admin/exports/portable`) to generate a compressed `.tar.gz` bundle. When `scrub=true` (`export_scrub` in UI), secret patterns and authentication tokens are actively redacted from prose fields before packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Admin Diagnostics & Realtime Probes Studio\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio (`/admin/diagnostics`) monitors subsystem health, executes active telemetry probes, and tracks container resource limits.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Admin Diagnostics Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `postgresql_rls` | PostgreSQL connectivity, pool saturation, migrations, RLS | 82 migrations, 100 tables `FORCE RLS` | Continuous | Core persistence and tenant isolation; latency threshold < 15ms |\n| `realtime_hub_outbox` | Transactional outbox relay and dispatch loop | Backlog depth, lag seconds, listener status | Continuous | Event delivery to WebSocket subscribers; lag threshold < 5s |\n| `job_worker_pool` | Background asynchronous task execution engine | Active tasks, worker concurrency, DLQ depth | Continuous | Processes asynchronous jobs; dead-letter queue must equal 0 |\n| `wasm_script_sandbox` | Wasmtime AST compiler and script sandbox execution | Compiler state, memory limits, fuel budget | Continuous | Sandboxed custom fields; fuel caps prevent infinite execution loops |\n| `agent_runtime_providers`| Health and latency of active AI model providers | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama` provider status | Continuous | Informs sparkle completions and AI gates; reports monthly budget |\n| `remote_runner_fleet` | Connected mTLS build runner nodes over port :8444 | Online runner count, heartbeat timestamp | Continuous | Distributed workload execution; heartbeats must arrive within 30s |\n| `rate_limiter` | In-memory token bucket and IP throttle health | Bucket capacity, blocked IP count, error rate | Continuous | Protects API endpoints from volumetric denial-of-service |\n| `realtime_pubsub_probe` | Active PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY latency measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 1.0ms) | On Demand | Validates database pub/sub bus responsiveness |\n| `websocket_ws_probe` | Real WebSocket ping/pong frame roundtrip measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 50ms) | On Demand | Validates client-to-server WebSocket bidirectional messaging |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`postgresql_rls`:** Evaluates database health via `GET /api/v1/admin/diagnostics`. Asserts that the single-head Alembic migration tree is fully applied (82/82 migrations), connection pool saturation remains under 75%, and all 100 application tables enforce `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Connection latency is sampled continuously with healthy thresholds < 15ms.\n- **`realtime_hub_outbox`:** Tracks the transactional outbox relay daemon (`database/outbox/relay.py`). Measures the unprocessed message queue depth, average dispatch lag (healthy < 5s), and PostgreSQL `LISTEN/NOTIFY` channel subscription status. Stalled outboxes trigger immediate administrative alerts.\n- **`job_worker_pool`:** Monitors asynchronous background task executors responsible for webhook dispatch, automated snapshot creation, and audit logging. Reports total active tasks, concurrency utilization, and dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth. Any non-zero DLQ count indicates unhandled job failures requiring inspection.\n- **`wasm_script_sandbox`:** Monitors the in-process Wasmtime runtime executing custom field formulas and transition helpers. Verifies compiler initialization, memory consumption caps (default 32 MiB / 33,554,432 bytes per instance), and fuel budget limits preventing CPU exhaustion.\n- **`agent_runtime_providers`:** Asserts the availability and response latency of registered AI model providers (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`). Reports monthly token consumption against configured budget ceilings, warning administrators before cloud budget caps halt automated transition gates.\n- **`remote_runner_fleet`:** Tracks external execution nodes connected via mutual TLS (mTLS) over dedicated port `:8444`. Reports active runner counts, certificate expiration dates, and heartbeat timestamps. Runners failing to report within 30 seconds are marked offline and their active leases rescheduled.\n- **`rate_limiter`:** Reports the runtime state of in-memory token bucket rate limiters across public and authenticated routes. Tracks blocked request volumes and active IP throttle mappings, ensuring system resilience against abusive traffic.\n- **`realtime_pubsub_probe` and `websocket_ws_probe`:** On-demand latency probes. The pub/sub probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`) injects a synthetic outbox notification and captures `LISTEN/NOTIFY` roundtrip time (typically ~0.38ms). The WebSocket probe initiates a live `/ws` binary ping frame in the operator's browser, measuring connection setup (~13.7ms) and ping/pong roundtrip latency (~0.44ms).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio\nThe Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`) inspects, classifies, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across all registered entities.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Link Auditor Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `audit_ids` | Flagged link audit record UUIDs selected for purge | List of 1 to 200 UUIDs | Mandatory in purge | Scope of destructive link deletion in single atomic transaction |\n| `reason` | Mandatory administrative justification for link purge | String (10 to 500 characters) | Mandatory in purge | Recorded in immutable activity ledger; rejects < 10 chars with 422 |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for inspecting audit record lifecycle | `unpurged`, `purged`, `resolved`, `all` | `unpurged` | Isolates active anomalies from resolved historical records |\n| `reason_filter` | Filters links by formal invalidity category | All 7 `InvalidLinkReason` codes | `ALL` | Isolates specific defect patterns for forensic inspection |\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`audit_scan`:** Triggered via `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit` (or the **Run Integrity Scan** button). Traverses `object_links` and returns summary counts across all 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` codes without acquiring table locks.\n- **`reason_filter`:** Allows administrators to filter detected anomalies across the 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` classes:\n  1. `DANGLING_SOURCE`: Source object UUID does not exist in the universal object spine.\n  2. `DANGLING_TARGET`: Target object UUID does not exist in the database.\n  3. `SOFT_DELETED_ENDPOINT`: One or both endpoints reference an object soft-deleted beyond the retention window.\n  4. `ARCHIVED_POLICY_VIOLATION`: Link connects active and archived entities violating project archiving rules.\n  5. `TYPE_MATRIX_VIOLATION`: Relation violates the registered link type matrix allowlist.\n  6. `CROSS_WORKSPACE_LEAK`: Link connects entities across disjoint workspace boundaries without federation.\n  7. `PERMISSION_SEVERED`: Endpoints span boundaries where caller or entity permissions have been severed.\n- **`audit_ids`:** The explicit list of 1 to 200 audit record UUIDs submitted to `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. Submissions with fewer than 1 or more than 200 items are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`reason`:** The mandatory rationale string required by `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. The string must contain at least 10 non-whitespace characters (e.g. `\"Decommissioning orphaned test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings containing fewer than 10 characters are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`status_filter`:** Selects the display subset in the Studio UI: `unpurged` (flagged items requiring operator intervention), `purged` (historically purged records), `resolved` (links where the underlying anomaly was repaired), or `all`.\n- **`immutable_audit_ledger`:** Every completed purge operation writes an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event to `activity_records`. The response returns `purged_count` and the list of `activity_ids` emitted to the ledger for forensic verification.\n"
